            {"san": "d3", "severity": "playable", "tier": "Foundation",
             "why": "The quiet move, and the one the top players use. It transposes straight back to the Pianissimo: Black plays ...Bc5 or ...Be7 and you get the slow game from the main line with none of the theory below.",
             "line": "Bc5 c3 a6 O-O"},
            {"san": "Nc3", "severity": "playable", "tier": "Foundation",
             "why": "The Four Knights. Symmetrical and completely safe — a legitimate way to sidestep every sharp line on this page.",
             "see": "fourknights"},
            {"san": "O-O", "severity": "playable", "tier": "Structure",
             "why": "Castling and letting Black take on e4 — playable, and a genuine concession, because getting the pawn back costs you the piece the whole opening is built around. 5.Bd5! Nf6 6.Bxc6 dxc6 7.Nxe5 levels the pawns at seven each and the engine calls the position dead level: you have traded the Italian bishop for a knight and bought Black a doubled c-pawn with it. What you must not do is reach for the pattern from the Four Knights: 5.Re1 d5 6.Bxd5?? Qxd5 7.Nc3 Nxc3! and you are a bishop down, because the knight that would recapture on d5 is the one standing on e4. If you play 5.Re1, answer 5...d5 with 6.Bb5.",
             "line": "Nxe4 Bd5 Nf6 Bxc6 dxc6 Nxe5"},
